{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Manx2 was a virtual commuter airline with its head office in Hangar 9, Isle of Man Airport in Ballasalla, Malew, Isle of Man.","title":"Manx2"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It sold flights and services from several airports in the UK with bases in Belfast City, Blackpool, Cardiff and Isle of Man.","title":"Manx2"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The flights were operated for Manx2 by a number of airlines including, Van Air Europe, FLM Aviation and Links Air.","title":"Manx2"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In December 2012 the assets of Manx2 were sold to Citywing and Manx2's last flight took place on 31 December 2012.","title":"Manx2"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"FLM Aviation was a German aviation company based at Kiel-Holtenau Airport.","title":"FLM Aviation"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was mainly flew executive charter flights, but also sightseeing, cargo and ambulance flights.","title":"FLM Aviation"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" At times FLM Aviation operated scheduled flights on behalf of dauair and for Manx2 to and from the Isle of Man.","title":"FLM Aviation"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Links Air was a British airline selling and operating scheduled regional flights as well as charter services.","title":"Links Air"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It formerly operated scheduled flights out of Doncaster Sheffield Airport and public service obligation flights in Wales from Cardiff to Anglesey on behalf of the Welsh Government.","title":"Links Air"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Air East was a commuter airline based at Johnstown–Cambria County Airport, Johnstown, Pennsylvania.","title":"Air East"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was established in 1967.","title":"Air East"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" On April 26, 1970, Air East inaugurated six daily passenger flights under the Allegheny Commuter brand.","title":"Air East"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 1974, Air East's commercial certificate was revoked by the FAA.","title":"Air East"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The airline operated Beechcraft Model 99 commuter turboprop aircraft.","title":"Air East"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Provincetown-Boston Airlines (IATA: PT, ICAO: PBA, Call sign: PBA) was an airline that operated between 1949 and 1989.","title":"Provincetown-Boston Airlines"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The airline operated a route network in New England, New York, Pennsylvania, and Florida, and at one time was the largest commuter airline in the United States before its purchase by People Express Airlines and then eventual consolidation with other commuter airlines into Continental Express, now United Express after its merger.","title":"Provincetown-Boston Airlines"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"GeorgiaSkies was an American commuter airline brand founded by Pacific Air Holdings to operate flights in Georgia after the airline was awarded an Essential Air Service contract to serve Athens and Macon, Georgia.","title":"GeorgiaSkies"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The airline started flights on September 29, 2008 and used the airline identifiers and call signs of its parent company Pacific Wings.","title":"GeorgiaSkies"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The airline was headquartered in Dallas, Texas.","title":"GeorgiaSkies"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Links Air
